<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qasim Amin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qasim Amin was an Egyptian jurist and Islamic modernist, a co-founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> University and often counted among the Arab world's first feminists. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, he took a law degree in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and studied at Montpellier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and from 1885 served in Egypt's courts. He criticised veiling, seclusion, early marriage and the lack of education for women, and his book The Liberation of Women, published in 1899, called for an end to the veil. His advocacy stirred debate across the Arab world, though scholars such as Leila Ahmed have questioned his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qasim-amin.jpg" alt="Qasim Amin" width="260" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qasim_Amin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Qasim Amin</b> and women writers such as Aisha Taymur and Zaynab Fawwaz called for the education of women and debated their place in society.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>In Germany Clara Zetkin built the social-democratic women’s movement; in 1899 <b>Qasim Amin</b>, called the “father” of Arab feminism, wrote The Liberation of Women; in 1923 Hoda Shaarawi founded the Egyptian Feminist Union; and the Iranian Constitutional Revolution of 1905 set off an Iranian women’s movement.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=1892" aria-label="Alexandria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5322 1027 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Qasim Amin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5772 1297h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=1892">What was here in 1892? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
